      "output": {
        "decision": "answer",
        "reason": "The evidence fully supports a comparison. Chunk c-bd28c872 describes four USSR machine translation approaches in the late 1950s (experimental, logical, information-theoretic/cybernetic, interlingual), noting the Lyapunov/Kulagina/Dobrushin cybernetic approach was the main role model for the Croatian efforts. Chunk c-4fb75837 states Croatian research lagged only a couple of years behind the superpowers, noting US prototype efforts from 1954, US paper collection in 1955, USSR efforts from 1955, and UK prototype from 1956. It also states that unlike US/USSR colleagues, Laszlo's group had no actual computer and results remained mainly theoretical, and that such research was a top-priority military effort in other countries. The Abstract also contrasts the Croatian cybernetic approach with usual logical approaches of the period. Together these directly answer the comparison question."
      },
